<template id="captions-template">
  <div data-composition-id="captions" data-width="1920" data-height="1080" style="position:absolute; top:0; left:0; width:1920px; height:1080px; pointer-events:none;">
    <style>
      @font-face {
        font-family: "Source Serif 4";
        src: url("../assets/fonts/SourceSerif4-Regular.woff2") format("woff2");
        font-weight: 400;
        font-style: normal;
      }
      @font-face {
        font-family: "Playfair Display";
        src: url("../assets/fonts/PlayfairDisplay-Bold.woff2") format("woff2");
        font-weight: 700;
        font-style: normal;
      }
      @font-face {
        font-family: "Playfair Display";
        src: url("../assets/fonts/PlayfairDisplay-Black.woff2") format("woff2");
        font-weight: 900;
        font-style: normal;
      }
      @font-face {
        font-family: "Roboto Mono";
        src: url("../assets/fonts/RobotoMono-Regular.woff2") format("woff2");
        font-weight: 400 700;
        font-style: normal;
      }
      #cap-stage {
        position: absolute;
        top: 0; left: 0;
        width: 100%; height: 100%;
        pointer-events: none;
        font-family: "Roboto Mono", "Courier New", monospace;
      }
      .cap-group {
        position: absolute;
        bottom: 32px;
        left: 50%;
        transform: translateX(-50%);
        max-width: 1500px;
        padding: 14px 32px;
        font-size: 36px;
        font-weight: 500;
        line-height: 1.25;
        text-align: center;
        color: #E8DEC8;
        letter-spacing: 0.02em;
        background: rgba(14, 11, 8, 0.88);
        border-top: 1px solid rgba(200, 117, 51, 0.6);
        border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(200, 117, 51, 0.6);
        backdrop-filter: blur(2px);
        opacity: 0;
        visibility: hidden;
      }
    </style>

    <div data-hf-studio-original-display="" data-hf-studio-original-box-sizing="" data-hf-studio-original-flex-shrink="" data-hf-studio-original-flex-grow="" data-hf-studio-original-flex-basis="" data-hf-studio-original-max-height="" data-hf-studio-original-max-width="" data-hf-studio-original-min-height="" data-hf-studio-original-min-width="" data-hf-studio-original-height="" data-hf-studio-original-width="" data-hf-studio-box-size="true" style="--hf-studio-width:1920px;--hf-studio-height:1080px;width:1920px;height:1080px;min-width:0px;min-height:0px;max-width:none;max-height:none;box-sizing:border-box" id="cap-stage">
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-0">The cartoon ran in every</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-1">Hearst paper in America.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-2">A fat man in a</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-3">suit covered in dollar signs</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-4">smoking a cigar the size</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-5">of his forearm.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-6">The caption changed every week.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-7">The man did not.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-8">His name was Mark Hanna,</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-9">and in 1896, he did</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-10">something no one had ever</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-11">done at that scale.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-12">He turned the American presidential</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-13">election into a corporate purchase</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-14">order.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-15">Three and a half million</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-16">dollars raised.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-17">20 times what his opponents</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-18">spent.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-19">Banks assessed at a quarter</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-20">of a percent of capital.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-21">Railroads shaken down personally.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-22">Standard oil, $250,000.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-23">J.P.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-24">Morgan, $250,000.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-25">Half a million voters transported</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-26">by train to shake one</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-27">man's hand.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-28">200 million pieces of campaign</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-29">literature printed and mailed.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-30">The opposition had a 36-year-old</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-31">preacher who could move a</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-32">crowd to tears.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-33">Hanna had a ledger.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-34">And when November came, the</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-35">ledger won.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-36">He wasn't born with money.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-37">He was born in a</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-38">grocery.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-39">New Lisbon, Ohio, 1837.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-40">His father, Dr.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-41">Leonard Hanna, was a country</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-42">physician who ran a wholesale</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-43">grocery on the side.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-44">Flour, salt, dry goods to</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-45">Ohio farmers.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-46">When Mark was 15, the</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-47">family moved to Cleveland.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-48">He enrolled at Western Reserve</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-49">College.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-50">In his class was a</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-51">quiet, awkward boy who would</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-52">grow up to control 90%</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-53">of the oil-refining capacity in</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-54">the United States.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-55">His name was John D.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-56">Rockefeller.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-57">The two would orbit each</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-58">other for the next 40</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-59">years.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-60">Hanna lasted one year at</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-61">Western Reserve before he quit,</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-62">not because he was failing,</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-63">because he had already figured</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-64">out something the professors couldn't</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-65">teach.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-66">The real education was in</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-67">the price of things.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-68">He went back to the</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-69">store, worked the floor, watched</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-70">how men negotiated, watched who</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-71">paid on time and who</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-72">didn't, watched which suppliers had</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-73">leverage and which ones begged.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-74">Then the Civil War came.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-75">He served briefly, came home,</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-76">and married a woman named</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-77">Charlotte Rhodes.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-78">Her father was Daniel Rhodes,</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-79">coal iron, one of the</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-80">men quietly getting rich off</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-81">everything Cleveland was becoming.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-82">Hanna walked into that business</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-83">and never walked out.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-84">By 1867, Marcus Alonso Hanna</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-85">had built M.A.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-86">Hanna and Company, a coal</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-87">and iron brokerage, sitting at</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-88">the exact pivot point where</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-89">Lake Erie ore boats unloaded</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-90">for the Pittsburgh Steel Mills.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-91">Cleveland was the choke point</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-92">and Hanna sat on it.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-93">Every ton of iron ore</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-94">that crossed Lake Erie in</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-95">the 1870s passed through brokers</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-96">like him.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-97">He took a cut.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-98">Then he bought the newspaper,</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-99">the Cleveland Herald, then a</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-100">theater, then banking interests, then</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-101">a streetcar line.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-102">By 1880, he controlled enough</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-103">of Cleveland that when he</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-104">walked into a room, the</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-105">room changed temperature and he</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-106">was bored.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-107">Not with money, he had</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-108">money bored with the ceiling</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-109">because Hanna had figured out.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-110">Watching the men who supplied</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-111">his coal, the politicians who</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-112">regulated his rail routes, the</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-113">judges who settled his contracts,</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-114">that money was the second</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-115">most powerful thing in America.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-116">The most powerful thing was</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-117">who decided where the money</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-118">went.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-119">That was politics and no</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-120">one had ever run it</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-121">like a business.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-122">Not yet.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-123">The year was 1888.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-124">The Republican National Convention, Chicago.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-125">Mark Hanna arrived not as</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-126">a candidate, but as a</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-127">kingmaker.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-128">His man was John Sherman.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-129">Ohio Senator, Treasury veteran, the</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-130">establishment choice.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-131">Hanna worked the convention floor</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-132">for six days trading delegates,</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-133">promising postmasterships, calling in markers</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-134">from Ohio to Pennsylvania.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-135">Sherman peaked on the first</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-136">ballot at 229 votes.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-137">Then he bled out ballot</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-138">after ballot until the nomination</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-139">went to Benjamin Harrison on</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-140">the eighth.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-141">Hanna lost, but he saw</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-142">something that weak he would</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-143">never forget.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-144">A 45-year-old congressman from Canton,</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-145">Ohio had been chairing the</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-146">resolutions committee.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-147">When delegates began breaking toward</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-148">him as a dark horse</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-149">alternative, the congressman walked to</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-150">the microphone and refused.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-151">He told the convention he</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-152">was bound to Sherman and</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-153">he sat down.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-154">That kind of loyalty was</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-155">rare in 1888 politics.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-156">It was almost extinct.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-157">His name was William McKinley.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-158">Hanna walked across the floor</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-159">and introduced himself.</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-160">He didn't know it yet,</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-161">but he had just found</div>
      <div class="cap-group" id="cap-162">his president.</div>
    </div>

    <script>
      window.__timelines = window.__timelines || {};
      const tl = gsap.timeline({ paused: true });

